            <ArticleTitle>Myoclonus-like involuntary movements following cesarean delivery epidural anesthesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>392-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2016.05.014</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0952-8180(16)30199-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spinal myoclonus following neuraxial anesthesia is rare. This report describes a case of myoclonus-like involuntary movement that occurred during the recovery from epidural anesthesia for a cesarean delivery. The patient's symptom improved with the administration of benzodiazepine, and the patient recovered with no neurological sequelae. In conclusion, epidural anesthesia can cause spinal myoclonus, which can be treated with a benzodiazepine.</AbstractText>
